## Deploy (infrastructure as code)

This project can provision cloud infrastructure and release the app with the
`dude iac` command group. The commands below are the **stable, target-agnostic
interface** — what each one *means*. For the concrete infrastructure this
project provisions (the cloud resources, sizing, and the end-to-end runbook),
see the **Deploy** page.

Every `iac` command is **scoped to one environment via `--env <name>`, which is
required** (there is no default — this prevents acting on the wrong environment
by accident). Run any `iac` command **without** `--env` to list the environments
discovered on disk. Most commands also accept `--profile <name>` to pick the
cloud credentials profile.

### Lifecycle

| Command | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `dude iac login --env <e>` | Configure/verify cloud credentials for an environment |
| `dude iac bootstrap --env <e> --state-bucket-prefix <p> --yes` | One-time: create the shared remote-state backend (+ container registries) |
| `dude iac new-env --env <name> [--from <env>]` | Scaffold a new environment by copying an existing one (default source: `dev`) |
| `dude iac init --env <e>` | Initialise the infra toolchain for the environment |
| `dude iac plan --env <e>` | Preview the infrastructure changes |
| `dude iac apply --env <e> [--yes]` | Create/update the infrastructure |
| `dude iac output --env <e> [--json]` | Show provisioned values (cluster, registry URLs, DB endpoint…) |
| `dude iac fmt --env <e>` | Format the infra source files |
| `dude iac validate --env <e>` | Validate the infra configuration |
| `dude iac destroy --env <e> [--yes]` | Tear the environment down |

### Release the application

| Command | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `dude iac kubeconfig --env <e>` | Point your local tooling at the provisioned cluster |
| `dude iac build --env <e> [--tag <t>]` | Build the backend + frontend production images |
| `dude iac push --env <e> [--tag <t>]` | Push the images to the registry |
| `dude iac deploy --env <e> [--tag <t>]` | Release the application to the cluster |
| `dude iac ship --env <e> [--tag <t>]` | Build + push + deploy in one step (the inner loop) |
| `dude iac status --env <e>` | Show the deployed release status and pods |

> Image tags default to the current git short SHA (with `-dirty` when the working
> tree has uncommitted changes). Pass `--tag <t>` to override — useful for
> promoting one already-built image across environments.

See the **Deploy** page for the full first-time runbook, how to add new
environments, and what gets provisioned.
{{/if}}

